        "title": "Historical Glassmaking - Egyptian Core-Formed, Roman, Venice/Murano, Bohemia",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "~3,500 BCE   Egyptian faience (silica+alkali — first glassy material; not true glass)\n~1,600 BCE   First true glass vessels — Egypt + Mesopotamia simultaneously\n             Core-formed technique; rare luxury (= precious stone value)\n~50 BCE      Glass blowing invented — Levantine coast (Sidon area)\n             → glass vessels affordable; Roman Empire fills with glass\n1st c. CE    Roman glass: mold blown, mosaic, cameo; extraordinary variety\n             Pompeii (79 CE) preserves extraordinary glass collection\n7th-13th c.  Islamic glass: wheel-engraved, enameled, lustered traditions\n12th c.      Venetian glass industry established (Byzantine craftsmen)\n1291         Murano: Venice moves glassmakers to island (fire safety + monopoly)\n~15th c.     Venetian cristallo: near-colorless glass revolution\n~17th c.     Bohemian potash-lime glass + wheel engraving dominates cutting\n1676         Ravenscroft: English lead crystal (high RI + softness → cuttable)\n1827         Chance Brothers continuous sheet glass (Crystal Palace, 1851)\n1959         Pilkington float glass → flat glass revolution\n```"
